# Migrating BackgroundProcessor to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed**
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** by the runtime

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Task didn't finish in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent of Abort(); you must design
            // GetNextItemAsync to respect the token.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort`. If `GetNextItem()` blocks indefinitely and ignores the token, the thread **cannot** be force-killed. You *must* make blocking calls cancellation-aware. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `volatile` flag can't interrupt a blocking `Read()` or `Wait()`. Pass `CancellationToken` into the blocking API itself. |
| **Catching `ThreadAbortException` is dead code** | It will never fire. Replace with `OperationCanceledException`. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort` → no equivalent needed** | Cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow, so there's nothing to reset. |
| **Sync-over-async risk** | If you keep synchronous `GetNextItem()`, wrap it to accept a token (e.g., use `Channel<T>.ReadAsync(token)` or polling with `token.WaitHandle`). |

## If You Must Keep Synchronous Blocking

```csharp
// Wrap a non-cancellable blocking call with a timeout/token check
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!_queue.TryDequeue(out var item))
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        Thread.Sleep(50); // poll interval
    }
    return item;
}
```

Or better — replace the queue with `System.Threading.Channels`:

```csharp
private readonly Channel<WorkItem> _channel = Channel.CreateBounded<WorkItem>(100);

// Naturally cancellable:
var item = await _channel.Reader.ReadAsync(token);
```

## Summary

The core principle: **.NET 8 uses cooperative cancellation**. There is no way to forcibly terminate work. Every blocking call in your loop must accept and honor a `CancellationToken`, or your `Stop()` will hang forever.
